+---
+id: task-005-createpatch-json-patch-of-the-changes-between-two-documents
+type: task
+title: "createPatch: JSON Patch of the changes between two documents"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+# createPatch: JSON Patch of the changes between two documents
+
+## Request
+
+Our clients want to send only what changed between two versions of a JSON document, instead of the whole new
+version. Add a way to produce such a change as a JSON Patch (RFC 6902): a function that takes the old and the
+new version and returns the patch that turns the first into the second. The patch describes what changed and
+nothing more: when two versions differ in one small place, sending the whole document again is not acceptable.
+
+## Scope
+
+- Export `createPatch(from, to)` from `src/index.ts`. It returns an array of JSON Patch operations (the
+  `PatchOperation` type of task-002) that, applied with `applyPatch` to `from`, yields a value equal to `to`.
+- Neither `from` nor `to` is mutated. The returned operations share no object or array with either input, so a
+  later change to the patch or to an input does not change the other.
+- Throw an error (`invalid JSON document: ...`, as `applyPatch` does) when `from` or `to` is not a JSON value.
+- Document the function with a doc comment that says why it is minimal in the way it is (see Design below).
+- Add a short section to `README.md`, next to the JSON Patch section, with an example.
+
+## Design
+
+- Objects: a member only in `from` gives `remove`; a member only in `to` gives `add`; a member in both is
+  compared recursively, at its own pointer. Members that are equal produce nothing.
+- Arrays: the common prefix and the common suffix of the two arrays (compared by JSON equality) produce nothing.
+  The middle is then compared element by element at the same index, and the surplus is removed from the end of
+  `from` or added at the end of `to`. So inserting or removing one element in a long array gives one operation.
+- Anything else that differs (a primitive, or a value whose type changes between object, array and primitive)
+  gives one `replace` at its pointer. A root that differs gives `replace` with the empty pointer `""`.
+- Pointer tokens are escaped as RFC 6901 requires (`~` as `~0`, `/` as `~1`).
+- Operations are emitted in an order that applies correctly in sequence: each one's index is valid in the
+  document as the earlier operations have left it.
+- The `JSON Patch` checks of task-003 apply to the output: `value` members hold JSON values taken as copies.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+- `createPatch(a, a)` returns `[]` for any JSON value `a`.
+- Applying `createPatch(from, to)` with `applyPatch` to `from` gives a value equal to `to`, for every pair of JSON
+  values in the tests (objects, arrays, nested values, primitives, and a root that changes type).
+- A single changed primitive member gives a single `replace` at its pointer; a single added or removed member gives
+  a single `add` or `remove`.
+- A single changed element deep in a document gives a single operation at its full pointer, and the unchanged
+  parts of the document are not in the patch.
+- An element inserted into or removed from the middle of a long array gives a single `add` or `remove` at its
+  index, and not a rewrite of the elements after it.
+- A member whose value changes type (for example from an object to a string) gives one `replace`.
+- A root that changes gives one `replace` with the path `""`.
+- Keys with `/` or `~` are escaped in the pointers (`/a~1b`, `/m~0n`).
+- The member `__proto__`, held as data, is handled as any other key.
+- Circular values, and values that are not JSON, are rejected with an error.
+- Neither input is mutated, and the result shares no object or array with either input.
+- Tests cover the happy paths and the error paths above, and run with `npm test`.
+
+## Requirements traceability
+
+- Feature: JSON Patch support for the json-toolkit library, in the direction of producing a patch.
+- Standard: RFC 6902 (JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) Patch), and RFC 6901 for the pointer tokens.
+- Depends on: task-001 (JSON Pointer, RFC 6901), task-002 (JSON Patch), task-003 (its checks of JSON values).
+- Consumers: the other team projects that import this library (see README.md), and the clients that send patches.
